(version 1)

# WHY THE 30 A WIDTH RULE IS NO LONGER HERE
#
# It used to say:
#     (constraint track_width (min 10.7mm))  on A.hasNetclass('HV_POWER')
# meaning "a 30 A conductor must be sized for 30 A". It could not do that job.
#
# HV_POWER matches +60V, GND, *SW_*, *COIL_* and *VB*. Every one of those nets
# also carries signal-level connections: a bootstrap capacitor to a driver VS
# pin, a gate-source resistor, a logic ground return. The rule demanded 10.7 mm
# of copper for all of them. It is what forced the router to a 10.7 mm track
# that could not fit between the board edge and its neighbour, and it would have
# fired on every GND track on the board the moment signal routing started.
#
# The thing it was trying to protect is pour width, and DRC cannot see pour
# width -- a zone is not a track. tools/pour_check.py measures it directly:
# it rasterises each outline at 0.05 mm and reports the narrowest cut across the
# conductor with the IPC-2221 rise that width implies at 30 A. As of 2026-08-27
# the four coil pours measure 10.70 to 11.00 mm, 19 to 20 C.
#
# So the check moved to where it can actually be performed. Run pour_check.py
# after any pour edit; DRC will not catch a narrow pour for you.

# docs/21 section 21.4: In1.Cu is a solid GND plane, no routing, ever.
# Vias must still pass through it, so only tracks are disallowed.
(rule "In1.Cu carries no tracks"
	(constraint disallow track)
	(condition "A.Layer == 'In1.Cu'"))
